CMPE 300 - Project 1
Mert Seyhan (2023400213), Enes Ersoy (2023400039)

FILES
  @PROGRAM@        the program (single file)
  @REPORT@        the report

REQUIREMENTS
  Python 3 (tested with Python 3.9.6, 3.12 and 3.14.3). Only the standard
  library is used; nothing has to be installed. Run the commands below from
  the folder that contains @PROGRAM@.

NORMAL MODE (timing experiment)
  python3 @PROGRAM@ 2023400213 2023400039

  Prints the variant line, then the execution time of the best, worst and
  average case for the 15 sizes 1, 5, 10, 20, 40, 50, 60, 80, 100, 150, 200,
  250, 300, 350, 400:

    Variant: 4 (p_a=2/5, p_b=2/5, p_c=1/5, d=2)
    Case: best Size: 1 Elapsed Time (s): ...
    ...
    Case: worst Size: 400 Elapsed Time (s): ...
    Case: average Size: 1 Elapsed Time (s): ...
        Runs (s): t1, t2, ..., t10
    ...

  Each "Case: average" line is the mean of 10 runs on random inputs
  (P(a)=0.4, P(b)=0.4, P(c)=0.2); the indented "Runs (s):" line below it lists
  the 10 individual times. Best and worst inputs are those of basic operation
  (4), which we identified as the correct one:
    best  = a^(n-L) c^L          with L = floor(log2 n) + 1
    worst = a^floor(n/2) b^ceil(n/2)
  Small inputs are repeated until at least 20 ms have passed (mean per run is
  reported), and every measurement takes the fastest of 3 such rounds.
  The total running time depends on the machine; on the Apple M1 laptop used
  for the report it was about 15-30 seconds.

COUNT MODE
  python3 @PROGRAM@ 2023400213 2023400039 --count <string>

  <string> may contain only the letters a, b and c. The algorithm is run once
  and only two lines are printed, for example:

    $ python3 @PROGRAM@ 2023400213 2023400039 --count abcabcba
    Variant: 4 (p_a=2/5, p_b=2/5, p_c=1/5, d=2)
    Count(1): 13 Count(2): 14 Count(3): 10 Count(4): 61

  Count(1): comparisons of lines 3 and 8
  Count(2): loop increments of the loops on lines 9 and 15
  Count(3): assignments of lines 4, 10, 14
  Count(4): assignments of lines 7, 12, 16

NOTES
  * The variant is computed from the two student numbers:
    key = (No1 + No2) mod 8, so the order of the two numbers does not matter.
  * The counters are only in the counted version of the algorithm, which is
    used only in count mode; timing uses a separate version without counters.
  * Wrong arguments print a usage message to stderr and exit with status 2.
  * Count mode runs the algorithm once, so its running time grows with the
    input like the algorithm itself (up to about n^3/4 operations for an input
    of length n).
